@entry cpp-hello
@also c++ cpp g++ hello-cpp
@header c++
@summary The first C++ program: build it with g++ inside rozOS.
@desc
C++ is C with classes, templates and a big standard library (strings,
vectors, maps, streams ...). rozOS runs GCC's C++ compiler, g++, after
    rozpkg install binutils gcc
Save the program as hello.cpp, then:
    g++ hello.cpp -o hello
    ./hello
In Code: File > New C++ file, then F5 (Code uses g++ for .cpp files).
In edit: Ctrl+R. Useful flags: -O2 (faster program), -std=c++20,
-pthread (for std::thread), -Wall (more warnings).
Everything in the standard library lives in the namespace std, so
std::cout is the "cout" of the library.
@see std::cout std::string std::vector class namespace
@example
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
    std::cout << "Hello from C++ on rozOS!\n";
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry std::cout
@also cout cin cerr endl std::cin std::cerr std::endl iostream std::getline
@header iostream
@syntax std::cout << value << value ...;
@syntax std::cin >> variable;
@syntax std::getline(std::cin, line);
@summary Print to the screen and read the keyboard with streams.
@desc
std::cout writes to standard output, std::cerr to standard error, std::cin
reads standard input. << and >> work for every built-in type and for
std::string, so there is no format string like printf's. '\n' ends a line;
std::endl does the same and also flushes the output (slower in loops).
std::cin >> word reads one word; std::getline(std::cin, line) reads a whole
line. In a condition a stream is true while reading works, so
    while (std::cin >> x) ...
reads until the end of the input.
@returns
The stream itself, so calls can be chained.
@see cpp-hello std::string std::stringstream printf
@input
41 rozOS
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main()
{
    int n;
    std::string name;
    std::cout << "number and name: ";
    std::cin >> n >> name;
    std::cout << "\n" << name << " + 1 = " << n + 1 << '\n';
    std::cerr << "(this line goes to stderr)\n";
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry std::string
@also std::to_string to_string std::stoi stoi std::stod
@header string
@syntax std::string s = "text";
@syntax s.size()  s += "more"  s.substr(pos, len)  s.find("x")
@summary A text string that grows by itself (no buffer sizes).
@desc
std::string owns its memory: adding text with + or += makes it longer, and
it is freed automatically. Useful members: size(), empty(), substr(pos,
len), find(text) (returns std::string::npos if not found), replace,
insert, erase, c_str() (a const char * for C functions like printf).
Compare with ==, <. std::to_string(42) turns a number into a string,
std::stoi("42") and std::stod("3.5") turn a string into a number.
UTF-8 (Bangla) text works, but size() counts bytes, not letters.
@see std::cout std::vector std::stringstream strings
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main()
{
    std::string s = "rozOS";
    s += " is " + std::to_string(64) + "-bit";
    std::cout << s << " (" << s.size() << " bytes)\n";
    std::string::size_type at = s.find("64");
    if (at != std::string::npos)
        std::cout << "found 64 at " << at << ", word: " << s.substr(0, 5) << '\n';
    int n = std::stoi("123") + 1;
    std::cout << n << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry std::vector
@also vector push_back
@header vector
@syntax std::vector<T> v;   v.push_back(x);   v[i]   v.size()
@summary A growing array: add items and it makes room by itself.
@desc
std::vector<int> holds ints, std::vector<std::string> strings, and so on.
push_back adds at the end, pop_back removes the last, v[i] reads or writes
item i (no bounds check; v.at(i) checks and throws), size() is the count,
clear() empties it, insert/erase change the middle. A vector can be
initialised with a list: std::vector<int> v{3, 1, 2}. Loop over it with a
range-for. The memory is freed automatically.
@see range-for std::sort std::map template
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main()
{
    std::vector<int> v{5, 3, 8};
    v.push_back(1);
    v[0] = 50;
    int sum = 0;
    for (int x : v) sum += x;
    std::cout << "items " << v.size() << ", sum " << sum << ", last " << v.back() << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry std::map
@also std::unordered_map unordered_map std::set
@header map
@syntax std::map<Key, Value> m;   m[key] = value;   m.count(key)   m.find(key)
@summary Key -> value tables (dictionaries), sorted or hashed.
@desc
std::map keeps its keys sorted; std::unordered_map (header <unordered_map>)
uses a hash table and is usually faster but unordered. m[key] gives the
value, creating it (0, "" ...) if the key is new. m.count(key) is 1 if the
key exists, m.find(key) returns an iterator (m.end() if not found),
m.erase(key) removes it. Looping gives pairs: p.first is the key,
p.second the value. std::set (header <set>) is a sorted set of keys only.
@see std::vector range-for auto-cpp
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <string>

int main()
{
    std::map<std::string, int> count;
    const char *words[] = {"apple", "pear", "apple", "fig", "apple"};
    for (const char *w : words) count[w]++;
    for (const auto &p : count)
        std::cout << p.first << ": " << p.second << '\n';
    std::cout << "has pear? " << count.count("pear") << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry std::sort
@also sort std::find std::reverse std::count std::max_element algorithm
@header algorithm
@syntax std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
@syntax std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), compare);
@summary Sort, search and count in containers (vector, array, string).
@desc
<algorithm> works on ranges given by two iterators: begin() and end().
std::sort sorts ascending, or by your own compare function (a lambda that
returns true if a comes before b). std::find returns an iterator to the
first match (end() if none), std::count counts matches, std::reverse turns
the order around, std::max_element / std::min_element find the largest or
smallest. Much easier than qsort.
@see std::vector lambda qsort
@example
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main()
{
    std::vector<int> v{7, 2, 9, 4};
    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
    for (int x : v) std::cout << x << ' ';
    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int a, int b) { return a > b; });
    std::cout << "| biggest first: " << v[0];
    bool has4 = std::find(v.begin(), v.end(), 4) != v.end();
    std::cout << " | has 4: " << has4 << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry class
@also constructor destructor this public private member-function
@header c++
@syntax class Name { public: Name(args); ~Name(); void f(); private: int x; };
@summary Classes: data and the functions that work on it, in one type.
@desc
A class bundles member variables and member functions. public members can
be used from outside, private ones only inside the class (struct is the
same but public by default). The constructor (same name as the class)
runs when an object is made; the destructor (~Name) runs when it goes away
- at the end of its block, or on delete. Inside a member function, this
points to the object. Member functions marked const promise not to change
the object. A constructor's ": x(a)" list initialises members.
@see virtual operator-overloading std::unique_ptr new
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Counter {
public:
    Counter(std::string name) : name_(name), n_(0) {}
    ~Counter() { std::cout << name_ << " done at " << n_ << '\n'; }
    void add(int k) { n_ += k; }
    int value() const { return n_; }
private:
    std::string name_;
    int n_;
};

int main()
{
    Counter c("clicks");
    c.add(2);
    c.add(3);
    std::cout << "value " << c.value() << '\n';
    return 0;                       /* the destructor prints here */
}
@end

@entry virtual
@also inheritance override polymorphism base-class
@header c++
@syntax class Derived : public Base { void f() override; };
@summary Inheritance: a class built on another, with virtual functions.
@desc
class Dog : public Animal makes Dog an Animal with extra parts. A virtual
function can be replaced (override) in the derived class; calling it
through a Base pointer or reference runs the derived version - that is
polymorphism. A base class with virtual functions should have a virtual
destructor. "= 0" makes a function pure virtual: the class is then
abstract and only derived classes can be created.
@see class std::unique_ptr
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <vector>

struct Shape {
    virtual ~Shape() {}
    virtual double area() const = 0;
};
struct Square : Shape {
    double s;
    Square(double side) : s(side) {}
    double area() const override { return s * s; }
};
struct Circle : Shape {
    double r;
    Circle(double radius) : r(radius) {}
    double area() const override { return 3.14159 * r * r; }
};

int main()
{
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Shape>> shapes;
    shapes.push_back(std::make_unique<Square>(2));
    shapes.push_back(std::make_unique<Circle>(1));
    for (const auto &s : shapes) std::cout << s->area() << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry operator-overloading
@also operator-cpp overloading
@header c++
@syntax Type operator+(const Type &a, const Type &b);
@summary Give operators (+, ==, <<) a meaning for your own types.
@desc
Write a function named operator+ (or operator==, operator<, operator[] ...)
and the operator works with your class. operator<< with std::ostream lets
std::cout print your type. Keep the meaning natural: + should add.
@see class std::cout
@example
#include <iostream>

struct Vec2 {
    double x, y;
};

Vec2 operator+(Vec2 a, Vec2 b) { return Vec2{a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y}; }
bool operator==(Vec2 a, Vec2 b) { return a.x == b.x && a.y == b.y; }
std::ostream &operator<<(std::ostream &o, Vec2 v) { return o << "(" << v.x << ", " << v.y << ")"; }

int main()
{
    Vec2 a{1, 2}, b{3, 4};
    std::cout << a + b << (a + b == Vec2{4, 6} ? " ok" : " ?") << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry template
@also typename generic templates
@header c++
@syntax template <typename T> T biggest(T a, T b);
@summary Templates: one function or class for many types.
@desc
A template is a pattern; the compiler makes a real function for every type
it is used with. std::vector<int> and std::map<std::string, int> are class
templates from the library. The type is usually found from the arguments
(biggest(3, 4) uses int), or given in <> (biggest<double>(3, 4.5)).
Templates live completely in headers.
@see std::vector class auto-cpp
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

template <typename T>
T biggest(T a, T b)
{
    return a > b ? a : b;
}

template <typename T>
struct Pair {
    T first, second;
    T sum() const { return first + second; }
};

int main()
{
    std::cout << biggest(3, 9) << ' ' << biggest(2.5, 1.5) << ' '
              << biggest(std::string("pear"), std::string("apple")) << '\n';
    Pair<int> p{20, 22};
    std::cout << p.sum() << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry namespace
@also using std-namespace
@header c++
@syntax namespace name { ... }    name::thing    using std::cout;
@summary Namespaces keep names apart (std::, your own).
@desc
The standard library is in namespace std. Your own code can use a
namespace too, so two libraries can both have a "draw" function.
"using std::cout;" lets you write cout alone; "using namespace std;"
does that for everything in std - fine in small programs, best avoided in
headers.
@see cpp-hello class
@example
#include <iostream>

namespace geometry {
    const double pi = 3.14159;
    double circle_area(double r) { return pi * r * r; }
}

int main()
{
    using std::cout;
    cout << geometry::circle_area(2.0) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry references
@also reference-cpp const-reference pass-by-reference
@header c++
@syntax void f(int &x);    void g(const std::string &s);
@summary References: another name for a variable (no pointer syntax).
@desc
int &r = x makes r another name for x: changing r changes x. A function
taking int & can change the caller's variable (like a pointer, but without
* and &). const std::string & passes a big object without copying it and
without allowing changes - the usual way to pass strings and vectors.
@see pointers functions std::string
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

void twice(int &x) { x *= 2; }
std::size_t length(const std::string &s) { return s.size(); }

int main()
{
    int n = 21;
    twice(n);
    std::cout << n << ' ' << length("rozOS") << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry lambda
@also closure lambda-capture
@header c++
@syntax [capture](params) { body }
@summary Small unnamed functions written right where they are used.
@desc
A lambda is a function made in the middle of an expression - handy for
std::sort, std::for_each or std::thread. The [] part says which outside
variables it can use: [x] copies x, [&x] uses x itself, [=] copies all,
[&] uses all by reference. It can be stored in auto or in std::function.
@see std::sort std::thread auto-cpp
@example
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main()
{
    std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    int total = 0;
    std::for_each(v.begin(), v.end(), [&total](int x) { total += x; });
    auto square = [](int x) { return x * x; };
    std::cout << "total " << total << ", 7 squared " << square(7) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry range-for
@also for-each auto-cpp
@header c++
@syntax for (const auto &item : container) { ... }
@summary Loop over every item of a container; auto picks the type.
@desc
"for (x : v)" visits every item of a vector, map, string or array.
auto lets the compiler write the type (in C++ auto means "deduce the
type", not the old C storage class). Use const auto & to read without
copying, auto & to change the items in place.
@see std::vector std::map for
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main()
{
    std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3};
    for (auto &x : v) x *= 10;          /* change in place */
    for (const auto &x : v) std::cout << x << ' ';
    std::cout << '\n';
    auto pi = 3.14;                     /* double */
    std::cout << pi << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry try
@also catch throw exception-cpp std::runtime_error std::exception
@header c++
@syntax try { ... throw std::runtime_error("why"); } catch (const std::exception &e) { e.what(); }
@summary Exceptions: report an error by throwing, handle it with catch.
@desc
throw stops the function and jumps to the nearest matching catch, cleaning
up local objects on the way (their destructors run). Library errors derive
from std::exception (header <stdexcept> has runtime_error, out_of_range,
invalid_argument ...); e.what() is the message. An exception nobody
catches ends the program. vector::at and std::stoi throw on bad input.
@see class std::string errors
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>

double divide(double a, double b)
{
    if (b == 0) throw std::runtime_error("division by zero");
    return a / b;
}

int main()
{
    try {
        std::cout << divide(10, 4) << '\n';
        std::cout << divide(1, 0) << '\n';
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
        std::cout << "error: " << e.what() << '\n';
    }
    std::vector<int> v(3);
    try { v.at(10) = 1; } catch (const std::out_of_range &) { std::cout << "out of range\n"; }
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry new
@also delete new[] delete[]
@header c++
@syntax T *p = new T(args);   delete p;   T *a = new T[n];   delete[] a;
@summary Make objects on the heap (prefer std::unique_ptr or std::vector).
@desc
new makes an object on the heap and runs its constructor; delete runs the
destructor and frees it. Arrays use new[] and delete[]. Forgetting delete
leaks memory, deleting twice crashes - so modern C++ uses
std::make_unique (freed automatically) or std::vector instead of raw new.
@see std::unique_ptr class malloc
@example
#include <iostream>

struct Point {
    int x, y;
    Point(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) { std::cout << "made\n"; }
    ~Point() { std::cout << "gone\n"; }
};

int main()
{
    Point *p = new Point(3, 4);
    std::cout << p->x + p->y << '\n';
    delete p;
    int *a = new int[5]();
    a[4] = 9;
    std::cout << a[4] << '\n';
    delete[] a;
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry std::unique_ptr
@also unique_ptr std::make_unique make_unique std::shared_ptr shared_ptr make_shared RAII
@header memory
@syntax auto p = std::make_unique<T>(args);    auto s = std::make_shared<T>(args);
@summary Smart pointers: heap objects that free themselves (RAII).
@desc
std::unique_ptr owns one object and deletes it when the pointer goes out
of scope - no delete needed. It can be moved (std::move) but not copied.
std::shared_ptr can be copied; the object is deleted when the last copy is
gone (use_count() tells how many). This idea - a resource tied to an
object's lifetime - is called RAII and is how C++ avoids leaks.
@see new class virtual
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <string>

struct File {
    std::string name;
    File(std::string n) : name(n) { std::cout << "open " << name << '\n'; }
    ~File() { std::cout << "close " << name << '\n'; }
};

int main()
{
    {
        auto f = std::make_unique<File>("a.txt");
        std::cout << "using " << f->name << '\n';
    }                                   /* closed here automatically */
    auto s = std::make_shared<File>("b.txt");
    auto t = s;
    std::cout << "owners: " << s.use_count() << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry std::ifstream
@also ifstream ofstream std::ofstream fstream std::fstream
@header fstream
@syntax std::ofstream out("file.txt");   out << "text";
@syntax std::ifstream in("file.txt");    std::getline(in, line);
@summary Read and write files with streams.
@desc
std::ofstream writes a file (it is created or emptied), std::ifstream reads
one; both work like std::cout and std::cin. The file closes when the
object goes away. "if (!in)" checks that opening worked. Open with
std::ios::app to add at the end. For binary data use .read/.write.
@see std::cout std::stringstream fopen
@example
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main()
{
    {
        std::ofstream out("notes.txt");
        out << "first line\n" << 42 << '\n';
    }
    std::ifstream in("notes.txt");
    if (!in) { std::cerr << "cannot open\n"; return 1; }
    std::string line;
    int n = 0;
    while (std::getline(in, line)) std::cout << ++n << ": " << line << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry std::stringstream
@also stringstream std::istringstream istringstream std::ostringstream ostringstream sstream
@header sstream
@syntax std::ostringstream o;  o << x;  o.str()     std::istringstream in(text);  in >> x;
@summary Build strings with << or take them apart with >>.
@desc
std::ostringstream collects output like std::cout, then .str() gives the
text - an easy way to format numbers into a string. std::istringstream
reads from a string like std::cin, e.g. to split a line into words or
numbers.
@see std::string std::cout
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>

int main()
{
    std::ostringstream o;
    o << "pi is about " << 3.14 << ", " << 7 << " days";
    std::string s = o.str();
    std::cout << s << '\n';
    std::istringstream in("10 20 30");
    int x, sum = 0;
    while (in >> x) sum += x;
    std::cout << "sum " << sum << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry std::thread
@also std::mutex std::lock_guard lock_guard std::async
@header thread
@syntax std::thread t(function, args...);  t.join();
@syntax std::mutex m;  { std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m); ... }
@summary Run functions at the same time (build with -pthread).
@desc
std::thread starts a function (often a lambda) in a new thread; join()
waits for it. Threads that change the same data must take turns: a
std::mutex, held by a std::lock_guard for the length of a block, makes
sure only one thread is inside. Build with:  g++ prog.cpp -o prog -pthread
(Code adds -pthread by itself). rozOS runs threads on one CPU for now.
@see lambda pthread_create pthread_mutex_lock
@example
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>

int main()
{
    int total = 0;
    std::mutex m;
    auto work = [&](int n) {
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);
            total++;
        }
    };
    std::thread a(work, 1000), b(work, 1000);
    a.join();
    b.join();
    std::cout << "total " << total << '\n';
    return 0;
}
@end

@entry std::chrono
@also chrono sleep_for std::this_thread::sleep_for steady_clock
@header chrono
@syntax auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
@syntax std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(100));
@summary Measure time and wait, with units (ms, s) checked by the compiler.
@desc
steady_clock::now() gives a time point; subtracting two gives a duration,
and duration_cast turns it into milliseconds, seconds ... sleep_for
(header <thread>) pauses the thread. Units like std::chrono::milliseconds
cannot be mixed up by accident.
@see std::thread clock_gettime nanosleep
@example
#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>

int main()
{
    auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(50));
    auto t1 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    auto ms = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(t1 - t0).count();
    std::cout << "slept about " << (ms >= 50 ? "50" : "less than 50") << " ms\n";
    return 0;
}
@end
